SQL Final Project – Chinook Database

I worked with the Chinook Database, a sample relational database that represents a digital music store. It includes tables for:
Customers (who make purchases)
Employees (sales agents and managers)
Invoices & Invoice Items (purchase records)
Tracks, Albums, Artists, Media Types, and Genres (the music catalog)
Playlists (curated music lists)

The database provides a realistic way to practice SQL joins, filtering, grouping, and aggregation while answering real-world business questions.

My goal was to practice working with joins, filtering, grouping, and aggregation to answer business-style questions.

Core Queries
1. Customers outside the USA
SELECT FirstName, LastName, CustomerId, Country
FROM chinook.Customers
WHERE Country <> 'USA';

2. Customers from Brazil
SELECT *
FROM chinook.Customers
WHERE Country = 'Brazil';

3. Invoices from Brazilian customers
SELECT inv.InvoiceId, inv.InvoiceDate, cust.FirstName, cust.LastName, inv.BillingCountry
FROM chinook.Invoices inv
LEFT JOIN chinook.Customers cust
  ON inv.CustomerId = cust.CustomerId
WHERE inv.BillingCountry = 'Brazil';

4. Employees who are Sales Support Agents
SELECT *
FROM chinook.Employees
WHERE Title = 'Sales Support Agent';

5. Unique list of billing countries
SELECT DISTINCT BillingCountry
FROM chinook.Invoices;

6. Invoices associated with each Sales Agent
SELECT inv.InvoiceId, emp.LastName, emp.FirstName, emp.Title
FROM chinook.Employees emp
JOIN chinook.Customers cust ON cust.SupportRepId = emp.EmployeeId
JOIN chinook.Invoices inv ON inv.CustomerId = cust.CustomerId
WHERE emp.Title LIKE '%Agent%';

7. Invoice totals with customer and sales agent details
SELECT inv.Total, cust.FirstName, cust.LastName, cust.Country,
       emp.FirstName AS AgentFirstName, emp.LastName AS AgentLastName
FROM chinook.Invoices inv
JOIN chinook.Customers cust ON inv.CustomerId = cust.CustomerId
JOIN chinook.Employees emp ON cust.SupportRepId = emp.EmployeeId;

8. Number of invoices in 2009
SELECT COUNT(*) AS Invoices_in_2009
FROM chinook.Invoices
WHERE InvoiceDate BETWEEN '2009-01-01' AND '2009-12-31';

9. Total sales in 2009
SELECT SUM(Total) AS TotalSales_2009
FROM chinook.Invoices
WHERE InvoiceDate BETWEEN '2009-01-01' AND '2009-12-31';

10. Tracks purchased with invoice line IDs
SELECT t.Name AS Track_Name, ii.InvoiceLineId
FROM chinook.Invoice_items ii
JOIN chinook.Tracks t ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId;

11. Purchased track name with artist
SELECT ar.Name AS Artist, t.Name AS Track, ii.InvoiceLineId
FROM chinook.Invoice_items ii
LEFT JOIN chinook.Tracks t ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
INNER JOIN chinook.Albums a ON a.AlbumId = t.AlbumId
LEFT JOIN chinook.Artists ar ON ar.ArtistId = a.ArtistId;

12. Tracks with album, media type, and genre
SELECT t.Name AS Track, a.Title AS Album, m.Name AS MediaType, g.Name AS Genre
FROM chinook.Tracks t
JOIN chinook.Albums a ON a.AlbumId = t.AlbumId
JOIN chinook.Media_Types m ON m.MediaTypeId = t.MediaTypeId
JOIN chinook.Genres g ON g.GenreId = t.GenreId;

13. Total sales made by each sales agent
SELECT emp.FirstName, emp.LastName, 
       ROUND(SUM(inv.Total), 2) AS Total_Sales
FROM chinook.Employees emp
JOIN chinook.Customers cust ON cust.SupportRepId = emp.EmployeeId
JOIN chinook.Invoices inv ON inv.CustomerId = cust.CustomerId
WHERE emp.Title = 'Sales Support Agent'
GROUP BY emp.FirstName, emp.LastName;

14. Most purchased media type in the USA
SELECT m.Name AS MediaType, COUNT(*) AS Purchases
FROM chinook.Customers c
JOIN chinook.Invoices i ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
JOIN chinook.Invoice_items ii ON i.InvoiceId = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN chinook.Tracks t ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
JOIN chinook.Media_Types m ON t.MediaTypeId = m.MediaTypeId
WHERE c.Country = 'USA'
GROUP BY m.Name
ORDER BY Purchases DESC;

Additional Questions I Explored:

These were queries I created myself to dig deeper into the Chinook data:

1. Rock genre tracks sold in Germany
SELECT COUNT(*) AS RockTracks_Sold, inv.BillingCountry
FROM chinook.Invoices inv
JOIN chinook.Invoice_items ii ON inv.InvoiceId = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN chinook.Tracks t ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
JOIN chinook.Genres g ON t.GenreId = g.GenreId
WHERE g.Name = 'Rock'
  AND inv.BillingCountry = 'Germany';

2. Most purchased media type in the US
SELECT m.Name AS MediaType, COUNT(*) AS Purchases
FROM chinook.Customers c
JOIN chinook.Invoices i ON c.CustomerId = i.CustomerId
JOIN chinook.Invoice_items ii ON i.InvoiceId = ii.InvoiceId
JOIN chinook.Tracks t ON ii.TrackId = t.TrackId
JOIN chinook.Media_Types m ON t.MediaTypeId = m.MediaTypeId
WHERE c.Country = 'USA'
GROUP BY m.Name
ORDER BY Purchases DESC;

3. Albums with the most tracks
SELECT a.Title, COUNT(t.TrackId) AS Track_amount
FROM chinook.Albums a
JOIN chinook.Tracks t ON a.AlbumId = t.AlbumId
GROUP BY a.Title
ORDER BY Track_amount DESC;

Key Takeaways:

Practiced filtering with WHERE, BETWEEN, <>, and pattern matching.
Learned how to use INNER JOIN, LEFT JOIN, and multiple-table joins.
Gained experience with aggregate functions like SUM, COUNT, and ROUND.
Explored real-world style questions about sales performance, customer behavior, and product trends.
